<Project>

    <!--
        THE ARCHITECTURE LEDGER — the policy the gate enforces, and the only place it is written.

        This is an MSBuild file rather than JSON for a measured reason: the gate runs as a
        RoslynCodeTaskFactory inline task, and that host cannot resolve System.Text.Json (verified — the
        compile fails with CS0234 on System.Text.Json) while it resolves System.Xml.Linq fine. Expressing the
        policy as MSBuild items means the ledger needs NO parser at all on the gate's side: MSBuild evaluates
        it and hands the task typed items. It also buys what JSON cannot hold — the reasoning below lives in
        comments beside the rule it justifies instead of in invented "why" string fields.

        `puck architecture` reads this same file as XML. One ledger, two consumers, nothing to drift.

        WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT CONTAIN: which layer each project is in. That is declared per-project as
        <PuckLayer>/<PuckKind> in the csproj itself, and the gate reads those declarations. A central table
        of project-to-layer would be a second source for a fact the csproj already states, which is a place
        for the two to disagree and nothing else.
    -->

    <!--
        SCOPE — a stated predicate, not an incidental path filter.

        A QUARANTINED TREE IS OUTSIDE THE GATE, because quarantine means ungated. This has to be said out
        loud: the repository root's Directory.Build.props reaches experimental/Puck.Demo (nothing stops
        MSBuild's upward walk there), so without this the gate would run on a project deliberately removed
        from the graph and fail it against a ledger that cannot describe it. Puck.BareMetal is already immune
        for a different and better reason — it carries its own Directory.Build.props/.targets firewall pair
        that stops both discovery walks — and that immunity is a property of ITS build, never of this list.

        A filter that happens to exclude the right directories reads identically to one that excludes the
        wrong ones, which is why the reason sits beside the path.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <PuckArchitectureGateEnabled Condition="'$(PuckArchitectureGateEnabled)' == '' AND $(MSBuildProjectFullPath.Contains('$([System.IO.Path]::DirectorySeparatorChar)experimental$([System.IO.Path]::DirectorySeparatorChar)'))">false</PuckArchitectureGateEnabled>
        <PuckArchitectureGateEnabled Condition="'$(PuckArchitectureGateEnabled)' == ''">true</PuckArchitectureGateEnabled>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
        THE LAYERS, top row first. Rank is the row index: a dependency must point at an EQUAL or GREATER
        rank, so a same-row edge is permitted and an upward one is not. This ItemGroup is the source for the
        project map's layering block rather than a restatement of it: `puck architecture map` generates that
        block from these rows and each project's own declaration.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Composition roots" Rank="0" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Validation" Rank="1" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Engine services" Rank="2" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Presentation" Rank="3" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Backends" Rank="4" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Shared substrate" Rank="5" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Leaf contracts and data" Rank="6" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        THE KIND AXIS — what a project IS, orthogonal to which row it sits on.

        A RANKED kind declares <PuckLayer> and obeys the rank rule at it. A TERMINAL kind must NOT declare
        one: it sits above every layer by construction, so a second declaration would only be somewhere for
        the two to disagree. Terminal kinds remain bound by every content rule below — a tool has no more
        business naming a GPU backend than an engine service does.

        A terminal kind is never a dependency OF A RANKED PROJECT. Terminal-to-terminal is fine, and that
        qualifier was earned rather than reasoned: stated flatly as "nothing may reference a terminal kind"
        the rule sounds exactly right, and the first run of the armed gate caught Puck.Analyzers.Tests
        referencing Puck.Analyzers as an ordinary assembly — a test suite that instantiates the analyzer and
        drives it over compilations it builds itself. The rule's purpose is that ENGINE CODE never depends on
        tooling; a test depending on the thing it tests is not that.

        Analyzer is worth its own row for a mechanical reason rather than a taxonomic one: an analyzer
        reaches every project through OutputItemType="Analyzer" with ReferenceOutputAssembly="false", so it
        never appears in a resolved reference set at all. The gate excludes it by construction, not by name —
        verified: Puck.Analyzers is absent from every project's @(ReferencePathWithRefAssemblies).
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="Production" Ranked="true" />
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="Validation" Ranked="true" />
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="Test" Ranked="false" />
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="Tool" Ranked="false" />
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="Analyzer" Ranked="false" />
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="FileApp" Ranked="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        THE BACKEND QUARANTINE, narrow form — and the narrowness is the whole of its value.

        A project may hold a Backends-row assembly in its resolved closure only if
          (a) its layer is Presentation or Composition roots; or
          (b) its kind is TERMINAL and it does not name a backend directly — a terminal consumer inherits
              the closure of whatever it composes and never introduces one. That inheritance is safe by
              COMPOSITION rather than by trust: every ranked project in the closure has passed this same
              gate in its own build, so a backend arriving through one arrived legally; or
          (c) it is named below.

        The wider form that suggests itself — "nothing may hold a Backends assembly unless it sits at or
        above that row" — permits any Engine-services project to take a direct backend dependency: legal by
        level, legal by closure, contradicting both the map's backend-neutrality rule and the entire reason
        the .Presentation wrapper row exists. That predicate would have flagged nothing, forever. Clause (b)
        is confined to terminal kinds for the same reason: extending closure-inheritance to ranked projects
        would reopen the hole through the .Presentation row, letting an engine service reach a backend by
        referencing a presentation wrapper.

        MEASURED, 2026-08-01, post-unit-2: exactly ONE exception across the settled graph. The absent finding
        is the load-bearing half — no Engine-services project reached a backend at all — so the quarantine was
        already real before it was armed, which is the best possible state to arm one
        in. Empty-because-the-rule-was-too-wide and empty-because-the-graph-is-clean are indistinguishable in
        a report and opposite in meaning; one honest exception is worth more than a clean sheet produced by
        not asking.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PuckArchitectureBackendException
            Include="Puck.Post"
            Reason="It must name BOTH backends to prove the same contract on each. That is the reason the quarantine exists rather than a breach of it — and it is a statement about Puck.Post's REFERENCES, unrelated to and outliving any temporary posture about whether Puck.Post currently builds."
        />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        LANE PROFILES — exact-equality closures for the assembly split.

        The listed closure must EQUAL the project's resolved Puck closure, not merely contain it, so REMOVING
        an edge is as visible as adding one and the split's edit stays a reviewable diff.

        This is the only tier doing security work. Every forbidden edge in the split is SAME-ROW, so the rank
        rule cannot see one of them: Puck.Scripting, Puck.Commands and Puck.Input all sit on Shared
        substrate, and an edge between them is downward-legal and architecturally fatal. Never manufacture a
        rank difference so the cheap check can do the expensive check's job — the rows describe what may
        depend on what, and bending them to smuggle in a security boundary corrupts both rules at once.

        Generated against the SETTLED graph after unit 2 landed (f0a54cda), never from a snapshot taken
        mid-flight.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <!-- The neutral channel/WASM core. Its whole purpose is the ABSENCE of Puck.Commands and
             Puck.Input; exact equality is what makes that absence enforced rather than merely current. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.Scripting" Closure="Puck.Assets;Puck.Maths" />
        <!-- The Simulation adapter — the one project permitted to hold the command vocabulary alongside the
             core. Puck.Abstractions arrives transitively through Puck.Input. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.Scripting.Simulation" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.Assets;Puck.Commands;Puck.Input;Puck.Maths;Puck.Scripting" />
        <!-- The headless P1 split (docs/project-map.md's Puck.World.Data row). The exact-equality closure is wider
             than Puck.World.Data's own direct references because Puck.Forge already needed
             Puck.HumbleGamingBrick and Puck.SdfVm before this split existed, and Puck.SdfVm in turn already needed
             Puck.Hosting/Puck.Recording/Puck.Shaders/Puck.Text — none of that is new here. Puck.Carriage joined
             2026-08-09: the admission handshake's identity door (Protocol/WorldAdmissionDoor.cs) verifies a
             connecting peer's signed-carriage claim through it — Leaf contracts and data, same row as
             Puck.Maths/Puck.Storage, so no layer boundary moves. What this profile
             actually enforces is the ABSENCE that matters: no Backends row, no Presentation row, no Puck.Overlays,
             no Puck.Input, no Puck.World.Server. A member that would need one of those (the engine-default binding
             document, the live command/channel vocabulary check) crosses through BindingVocabularyHook instead — an
             injection seam the composition root wires with a module initializer — rather than a direct reference.
             Puck.Scripting left this closure with the lane-axis deletion (owner ruling, 2026-08-02, the L5
             landing): WorldAddonRow no longer carries AddonLane, so nothing in this project names a Puck.Scripting
             type any more — Puck.World.Server (below) is the one row that still needs it, for the addon host
             itself. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.World.Data" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.Assets;Puck.Carriage;Puck.Commands;Puck.Forge;Puck.Hosting;Puck.HumbleGamingBrick;Puck.Maths;Puck.Recording;Puck.SdfVm;Puck.Shaders;Puck.Snapshots;Puck.Text" />
        <!-- The headless P1 split's server row (docs/project-map.md's Puck.World.Server row) — everything
             Puck.World.Data's profile carries (Server references Data), plus Puck.Scripting.Simulation's own
             closure (Puck.Input, Puck.Scripting.Simulation itself) and Puck.Storage (the owned-world cloud sync
             substrate WorldOwnedWorldSync reads through). Puck.Carriage also arrives directly (WorldTcpHost/
             WorldTcpWireFormat frame the admission handshake's envelope bytes). Same absence this profile actually
             enforces: no Backends row, no Presentation row, no Puck.Overlays. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.World.Server" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.Assets;Puck.Carriage;Puck.Commands;Puck.Forge;Puck.Hosting;Puck.HumbleGamingBrick;Puck.Input;Puck.Maths;Puck.Recording;Puck.Scripting;Puck.Scripting.Simulation;Puck.SdfVm;Puck.Shaders;Puck.Snapshots;Puck.Storage;Puck.Text;Puck.World.Data" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        The split's THIRD project — the presentation driver denied both Puck.Commands and Puck.Input — does
        not exist yet; it arrives with the four-surface retrofit. A profile for a project that does not exist
        cannot be verified and would be prose wearing a schema, so it is named here and becomes a
        PuckArchitectureProfile the moment the project lands.
    -->

    <!--
        INTERNALS-VISIBLE-TO, declared-vs-actual.

        The gate's declarations here are checked against each assembly's ACTUAL friend set read from the
        compiled PE, because scanning declarations trusts the thing being checked. Reading the PE is also the
        only form that sees both shapes this repository has used — [assembly: InternalsVisibleTo] in
        Properties/AssemblyInfo.cs, and <InternalsVisibleTo> csproj items — and a scan of either form alone
        returns a confident, wrong answer, measured by getting it wrong. Only the attribute form survives
        today; the PE read is kept because the csproj form costs one line to reintroduce.

        THE POLICY THIS TABLE ENFORCES: internals-visible-to is not endorsed here, and a TEST project is the
        only comfortable exception. Anywhere else, a member reached across a grant should be public instead —
        publicity is reviewable, a grant is a standing privilege nobody re-reads. So the shortness of this
        list is the point of it, and every row that is not a test project owes the reason it is not public.

        Puck.World HAD two friends — the file-based audio proofs — and they were the most privileged
        consumers in the tree, holding internals access to a composition root. They were also exactly what an
        analyzer-hosted gate could not have seen, since analyzer injection is off for $(IsFileBasedProgram),
        and a gate whose coverage silently excludes its most privileged consumers is worse than no gate. That
        argument is why the gate hooks where it does and is kept for it. Both grants are GONE as of the
        2026-08-02 quarantine: the scripts left the build, so the privilege was dead config that this ledger
        would have kept blessing. If those proofs are ever re-homed in a real project, the grant is re-issued
        to THAT assembly and named here — never restored to a file name.

        Gone with them: every grant naming Puck.Post (Puck.Maths, Puck.Platform and Puck.Scripting each held
        one), privilege pointing at an assembly the build no longer produces. Puck.Platform and
        Puck.Scripting held nothing else once Puck.Cli's grant became publicity on
        Puck.Scripting.WasmStdlibSources, so both AssemblyInfo.cs files are deleted rather than emptied and
        neither project appears below at all.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PuckArchitectureFriends Include="Puck.Carriage" Friends="Puck.Carriage.Tests" />
        <PuckArchitectureFriends Include="Puck.Maths" Friends="Puck.Maths.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
